WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.695
Permission models vary, and secure C code
should not pretend that every operating

2
00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:06.235
system behaves the same way.

3
00:00:06.315 --> 00:00:10.206
POSIX-style systems commonly describe
permissions through owner, group,

4
00:00:10.286 --> 00:00:11.320
and other mode bits.

5
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.683
Those bits express read, write, and
execute style permissions,

6
00:00:14.763 --> 00:00:19.020
but even on POSIX-like systems the real
result can be affected by process identity,

7
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.444
groups, default masks, mount options, and
file system behavior.

8
00:00:22.524 --> 00:00:25.868
Access control lists can add more detail
beyond simple mode bits.

9
00:00:25.948 --> 00:00:30.083
They may grant or deny access to specific
users or groups in ways that are not fully

10
00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:31.987
visible from a simple mode display.

11
00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:36.262
A program that checks only one simplified
view of permissions may miss policy that

12
00:00:36.342 --> 00:00:37.923
the system actually enforces.

13
00:00:38.003 --> 00:00:39.645
Windows uses a different model,

14
00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:43.252
commonly based on security descriptors and
access control entries.

15
00:00:43.332 --> 00:00:46.494
Portable C code should not assume POSIX
behavior everywhere.

16
00:00:46.574 --> 00:00:49.006
If an application targets multiple
platforms,

17
00:00:49.086 --> 00:00:52.430
the design should state which permission
concepts are required,

18
00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:56.888
which are platform-specific, and which
operations need separate implementations.

19
00:00:56.968 --> 00:00:58.792
Runtime environment also matters.

20
00:00:58.872 --> 00:01:02.095
Containers, services, scheduled jobs,
network file systems,

21
00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:05.823
removable media, deployment scripts,
inherited directory settings,

22
00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:09.612
and managed storage platforms can all
affect the final access decision.

23
00:01:09.692 --> 00:01:13.279
A mode value in source code is only one
part of the operational picture.

24
00:01:13.359 --> 00:01:16.096
Textbook examples are useful for learning
concepts,

25
00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:19.641
but production behavior is often shaped by
the surrounding system.

26
00:01:19.721 --> 00:01:23.552
Good documentation states the permission
model the application expects,

27
00:01:23.632 --> 00:01:25.031
the platforms it supports,

28
00:01:25.111 --> 00:01:28.576
and the assumptions that
security-sensitive workflows depend on.

29
00:01:28.656 --> 00:01:32.791
That documentation helps reviewers and
operators validate the real deployment

30
00:01:32.871 --> 00:01:35.060
instead of relying on memory or guesswork.
